From 0c017c680dff3ef9e2e739a1f5fa13d3c8398d6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 17:06:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] perf(dspark): copy drafts as the one behaviour of greedy
 adaptive verification (#842)

Base: candidate-37 (vLLM patch head 5b7cfa37f, tree af0fb152) plus #856
(8bb508091: sync_slot_draft_limits waits for the limit copy, #852's fix).

Copy drafts are no longer an option. SpeculativeConfig.copy_drafts is derived
(a field the constructor does not take, so --speculative-config refuses it):
CopyDraftsConfig's defaults (minimum match 16, 8 to 16 drafts) wherever DSpark
verifies greedy drafts adaptively. They are off only where the engine cannot
carry them: without async scheduling on the V2 model runner (VllmConfig), or
with a drafter checkpoint that has no confidence head, so no adaptive
verification (the runner; it raised before). The request-boundary checkpoint
and pipeline-parallel refusals go: the first never holds for DSpark, and
adaptive verification refuses the second itself.

VllmConfig fits a recipe sized for DSpark's uniform decode rows to the copy
envelope (max_decode_rows, one request verifying 16 drafts): with aligned
prefill chunks, a token budget of the chunk threshold beside the uniform rows
grows by the copy rows (6,144 to 6,155 at TP3, 16,384 to 16,395 at TP4; the
encoder budgets with it), the threshold and so every chunk boundary unchanged;
a CUDA-graph range ending at the uniform rows extends to the envelope on the
default ladder's stride (48 to 56, 59; 96 to 104, 107). That is the
configuration candidate-36 qualified as its copy arms (tp3-copy.json,
copy-tp4.json), which the plain recipe now gets.

A step's copy work runs once, beside DSpark. copy_alloc updates the copy state
from the step's verification and records the choice (source, offered drafts,
next drafts, acceptance estimate) beside the allocation and the slot's valid
draft count; copy_apply writes the proposals from that choice once DSpark has
drafted. copy_select, which repeated the chunk reduction and the state update
after DSpark, is removed. prepare_alloc runs copy_match and copy_alloc on the
output copy stream after postprocess, beside DSpark's draft forward, and the
allocation's copy to the host follows them there; propose makes the main stream
wait for them. With #856 the host's limits stay the latest proposal's: the limit
copy follows copy_alloc on that stream, and sync_slot_draft_limits waits for it.
A copy_match block whose share of the context is empty writes -1 without loading
the key.

Quality: no change to what is committed (drafts only change how many tokens a
step verifies), and the proposals, allocations, slot limits and copy state are
bit-identical to candidate-37's kernels step by step.

Tests: tests/v1/spec_decode/test_copy_drafts.py (the kernels through copy_alloc
and copy_apply against the numpy references, every chunk written, CUDA-graph
replay, the design's walk, temperature-0 text; the side-stream run equal to the
drafter-stream run with the side stream held busy, which fails without
propose's wait; and, #856's guarantee, the host limits after a busy side
stream's output copy equal the latest proposal's device limits);
tests/v1/core/test_copy_drafts_scheduler.py (refused as an option, derived on
and off, the budget and capture range widened at TP3 and TP4 shapes, off without
async scheduling).

Upstream: local; the copy drafter is this deployment's.
